#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281353 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ u-\\frac{1}{3}v\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ u-\\frac{1}{3}v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3{u}^{2} & -v \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3{u}^{2})\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-(1)\\cdot (-v) \\\\  & =v-{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{u}^{2} & <0 \\\\ v & <{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/13/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281354 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4u+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-{u}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4u+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-{u}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2v \\\\ -2u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-(-2u)\\cdot (2v) \\\\  & =4+4uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+4uv & >0 \\\\ 4uv & >-4 \\\\ uv & >-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v>-\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v<-\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n  \n\n![](images/c5/c501e7e341af57e86f18d73871314800d453f79572e16ccf2477335352d4dcdc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281360 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{uv} \\\\ {u}^{2}+v\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{uv} \\\\ {u}^{2}+v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v{e}^{uv} & u{e}^{uv} \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v{e}^{uv}\\cdot (1)-(2u)\\cdot u{e}^{uv} \\\\  & ={e}^{uv}(v-2{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv}(v-2{u}^{2}) & >0 \\\\ v-2{u}^{2} & >0 \\\\ v & >2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(0,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/61/613e83ce5ad6154905ec9001ece078309e396561e251e85455ff261bb4cd61a0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281366 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{u}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}v\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & -1 \\\\ 2uv & {u}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\right)\\cdot ({u}^{2})-2uv\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1+2uv \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,2)} & =(-1+2uv)\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-1+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281367 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(4,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{u}+{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & 2v \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(4,2)} & ={\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2}\\,|}_{u=4\\,v=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4}-2(2)^{2} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281368 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(0,3)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}u-{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & -2v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-2v) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(0,3)} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v)\\,{|}_{u=0\\,v=3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2(3) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|6|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =6\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281371 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,4)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2u}{v} & -\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2u}{v}\\cdot u-v\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{2}}{v}+\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,4)} & =\\left(\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v}\\right)\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281372 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(1,4)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{uv} \\\\ u{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 2uv\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2uv-{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\  & =v\\sqrt{uv}-\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv})\\,|}_{u=1\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)\\sqrt{(1)(4)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|4|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =4\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281377 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,\\,\\,y=t(5-t),\\,0\\le t\\le 5\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x={t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ | $25$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t(5-t).$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ | $25$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | $6$ | $6$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/1a/1aa848f287660c0858d5d6478da71c5baab9252c13f0a2d4a48c38d61f6bce51.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281382 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=3\\sqrt{t},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{3}-2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=3\\sqrt{t}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $1.25$ | $1.5$ | $1.75$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1.5$ | $2.12$ | $2.60$ | $3$ | $3.35$ | $3.67$ | $3.97$ | $4.24$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y={t}^{3}-2t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $1.25$ | $1.5$ | $1.75$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1.5$ | $2.12$ | $2.60$ | $3$ | $3.35$ | $3.67$ | $3.97$ | $4.24$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-0.48$ | $-0.88$ | $-1.08$ | $-1$ | $-0.55$ | $0.38$ | $1.86$ | $4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/88/8897c9d1d91007a51d715d982e6b1137bdc5f83f6ef89802b4115522809d4db5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281384 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ This gives\n\n| **$\\theta $** | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ | $\\pi $ | $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $7$ | $\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $-7$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$ This gives\n\n| **$\\theta $** | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ | $\\pi $ | $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $7$ | $\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $-7$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/de/debf9475589e7e242df986d1fffb8a97834d6ce550279fc82b0428001b661eaa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281385 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-2t,\\,\\,\\,y=3-t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the range of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}-2t \\\\  & ={(t-1)}^{2}-{(1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(t-1)}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${(t-1)}^{2}\\ge 0,$ we can conclude that $x\\ge -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281386 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5t-{t}^{2},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5t-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-({t}^{2}-5t) \\\\  & =-{(t-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(t-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${(t-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}\\ge 0,$ we can conclude that $x\\le \\frac{25}{4}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that ${t}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Consequently, $y\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x\\le \\frac{25}{4}$ and $y\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281416 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t)+0\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}(2t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(2t)}{2}]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281421 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,z={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}(t))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}(t))+{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(\\pi t)+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}(t)+2{t}^{3} \\\\  & =1+2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(1+2{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[t+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =((\\pi -(-\\pi ))+\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}-(-\\pi )^{4}}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281422 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=(1-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=1-{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1-{t}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (-2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2t)+0\\cdot 2t+\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2t+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2t+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi t))\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-{t}^{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1-\\frac{1}{4})-(-1-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281424 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=-z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =-z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t+(-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t)+\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t. \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[t]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281436 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{3}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-2t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}+3t,\\,z={t}^{3}-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(6{t}^{2}-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(6t+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(({t}^{3}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ((6{t}^{2}-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(6t+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t)\\cdot (6{t}^{2}-2)+0\\cdot (6t+3)+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =6{t}^{5}+9{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(6{t}^{5}+9{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{6}+\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{9}{5}+\\frac{1}{4}+1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281438 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t))+0\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)+\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)-\\pi {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)-\\pi {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}(2\\pi t)))\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}(\\pi t)}{2}+\\frac{\\pi t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-[0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0-(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0)] \\\\  & =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281453 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x-y=1,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}-x,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,$ the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}-x,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}-x,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{-x}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{1}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-(-x)\\cdot (4y)+1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d84b3ecc79c58a128d2f7ea262692d9f66dd9882ff244fb7c3dadd2b2571b550.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281454 :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨-x{e}^{z},\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{z}⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-x{e}^{z},\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{z}⟩{|}_{z=4} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-x{e}^{4}}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{{e}^{4}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-x{e}^{4})-0\\cdot 0+{e}^{4})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ with radius $2$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{4}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={e}^{4}[\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{r=0}^{r=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}(2\\pi -0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi {e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281457 :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}x⟩,$ and $C$  is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=x.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=x.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=x.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}x⟩{|}_{z=x} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{0}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{x}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{x}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot 1-x\\cdot 0+x))\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281499 :question/problem "![](images/58/581ac462bfcfead22a6c5ad4eb247f05adb1a677e92cac51954b0746cd083ab7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=⟨x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}2x-3z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2x-3z) \\\\  & =1+1+(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}-1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-\\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281500 :question/problem "![](images/aa/aac27058402f89bef73700129a7812b3cf4f971c11975fc1711a46b8d10e61fe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=⟨3x+y{e}^{z},\\hspace{0.222em}xy+2,\\hspace{0.222em}1-xz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+y{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+2)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1-xz) \\\\  & =3+x+(-x) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}3\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281501 :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-4)^{2}=4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+6y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(x+3z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+3z) \\\\  & =0+6+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}9\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =96\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281502 :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by $(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xz) \\\\  & =-2x+4+2x \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $1$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}4\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281503 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z={x}^{2}+y.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{x}^{2}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =1+(-2)+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}y}}}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{x}^{2}+y}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{x}^{2}-1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281504 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and $y=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=({y}^{3}-z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+0+2xy \\\\  & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}2xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le x\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le z\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2xy}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281505 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=4,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz) \\\\  & =2xy+0+xy \\\\  & =3xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}3xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}3\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4}\\cdot [3z]_{z=1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (8-0)\\cdot (6-3) \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281506 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=e,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=y\\operatorname{ln}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(x-{y}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{x}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{x}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}x]_{x=1}^{x=e}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot [z]_{z=0}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}e-\\operatorname{ln}1)\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2})\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281507 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(x+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x+2+0 \\\\  & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(2x+2)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x+2)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}+2x]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+2(1)-0)\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281508 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=-1,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+2xy+2xy \\\\  & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}4xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}4xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281554 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+t,\\,y={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,z=2{t}^{2}+2t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(4t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ((2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(4t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})(2t+1)+(2{t}^{2}+2t)(3{t}^{2}+2t)+({t}^{2}+t)(4t+2) \\\\  & =2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}+\\frac{11}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{17}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{8}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{11}{3}+\\frac{17}{4}-\\frac{8}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281555 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=({t}^{3}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2{t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,z={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2{t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3{t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =((2{t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ((3{t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)(3{t}^{2}-1)+3(4t)+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)(2t) \\\\  & =-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-t+5{t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}+\\frac{6}{5}{t}^{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{6}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1+5+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3})-(1+5-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{12}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281556 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{z-2y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{z-2y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)-2\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t))-2\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)+1\\cdot 2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}(\\pi t)-2\\pi {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(\\pi t)+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[-2\\pi t+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2\\pi (1)+0)-(-2\\pi (-1)+0) \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284400 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $5t\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=5t,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=5(2)=10\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=5(4)=20\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1.5\\cdot (20)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1.5\\cdot (10)^{2} \\\\  & =300-75 \\\\  & =225\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284401 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n2=\\sqrt{0}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\sqrt{t}+t+2.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=\\sqrt{1}+1+2=4\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=\\sqrt{4}+4+2=8\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {8}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =192-48 \\\\  & =144\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284402 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $8\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 1$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ when $t=1\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n2={1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{1}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{t}.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)={2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =81-16 \\\\  & =65\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284403 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(4-{x}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $2.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $(4-{x}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=2.5\\cdot (4-{x}^{2})=(10-2.5{x}^{2})\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(10-2.5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[10x-\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(10(1)-\\frac{5}{6}({1}^{3}))-(10(0)-\\frac{5}{6}({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =10-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{6}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284404 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $6\\sqrt{x}\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}6\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =4(8-1) \\\\  & =28\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 28 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ 28 & =\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{1}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}_{1}^{2} & =32 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =64 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $8\\,m/s.$"}]}
